WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.334
-Our first guest tonight
is a journalist you know

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:03.868
from Showtime's "The Circus,"

00:00:03.868 --> 00:00:05.534
"CBS Saturday Morning"
and "The Mole."

00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:08.400
She is the host of "Alex Wagner
Tonight," which airs

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.701
Tuesdays through Fridays
on MSNBC.

00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:13.567
Please welcome to the show
Alex Wagner, everybody!

00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:15.167
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:21.834
♪♪

00:00:21.834 --> 00:00:28.534
♪♪

00:00:28.534 --> 00:00:30.067
It's so nice to have you here.

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.534
-It was so nice to take the
elevator up a few floors.

00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:34.534
-It's lovely to have you back
in the building.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:37.734
You're hosting the 9:00 p.m.
hour on MSNBC.

00:00:37.734 --> 00:00:39.667
-I am.
-Tuesdays to Fridays.

00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:41.133
One of the really exciting
things about it is

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:45.067
you're the only Asian American
hosting a cable prime-time show.

00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:48.067
Is that something that is both,
I don't know, is it humbling?

00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:50.167
Is it frustrating
that it's true?

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:52.000
-Yes, it is both of
those things.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.601
It's absolutely humbling to be
representing a huge diaspora

00:00:56.601 --> 00:00:59.767
that is definitely
not all within me.

00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:02.934
I mean, Asian Americans are
wildly diverse.

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:05.300
I'm so proud to be
Burmese American.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.968
I'm so proud to have the
heritage that I do, and I try

00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:09.934
and do my best to,
you know, represent

00:01:09.934 --> 00:01:12.300
that part of the world
on our show.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.434
But it's insane that
in the year 2023,

00:01:15.434 --> 00:01:19.334
I'm the only Asian American
prime-time cable news host.

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:20.567
That should not be.

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:22.901
-Well, hopefully you're
the first of many.

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:24.400
Hopefully this is the beginning
of something.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.734
But I also want to ask,
you've hosted a show before,

00:01:26.734 --> 00:01:29.901
but this is a big one.
-Yeah.

00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:31.367
-It must make it a little bit
easier because

00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:33.634
you have a great many friends
over at MSNBC.

00:01:33.634 --> 00:01:36.634
Including you threw up a tweet
of you and Chris Hayes.

00:01:36.634 --> 00:01:38.934
So you and Chris have known
each other 25 years?

00:01:38.934 --> 00:01:41.400
-Oh, my God, and we're only 27.
-That's so weird.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.767
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:44.434
Like, from a very young age.

00:01:44.434 --> 00:01:47.367
We've known each other since,
like, acne days.

00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:48.601
-Really?
-That's how long --

00:01:48.601 --> 00:01:50.434
-You saw Chris Hayes with acne?

00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:52.567
-I'm not saying
whose acne it was.

00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:55.634
I'm just saying that's how
long -- our hormones were in a

00:01:55.634 --> 00:01:59.167
different place, and now they're
going into another place.

00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:01.067
But yeah, we've known each other
since college.

00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:05.100
And I remember -- he's a year
younger than me, and when he got

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.234
into our college, Brown, I
remember the upperclassmen were

00:02:09.234 --> 00:02:11.467
like, "Chris Hayes is
coming to Brown."

00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:12.968
And I was like, "Who is this
Chris Hayes?"

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:14.434
And now I know
what the fuss was about.

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:16.467
-Chris Hayes was so cool,
people at college

00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:17.868
knew him before he got there?

00:02:17.868 --> 00:02:20.200
-Let's say -- is it coolness?
I don't know.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.434
Notoriety. I'm just going to say
notoriety.

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:24.200
I can say that as someone who
loves him deeply

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.033
and has known him
a very long time.

00:02:26.033 --> 00:02:28.534
-You received some advice,

00:02:28.534 --> 00:02:30.601
and I received the same thing
when I started this show.

00:02:30.601 --> 00:02:31.868
And again, you've had
your own show before,

00:02:31.868 --> 00:02:33.267
so I know it's a little bit
different.

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:35.901
But how long it takes to be
comfortable

00:02:35.901 --> 00:02:37.067
behind the desk of
your own show.

00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:38.901
I know you've just started this.
-Yeah.

00:02:38.901 --> 00:02:40.534
-But do you feel like you're
there yet

00:02:40.534 --> 00:02:42.567
and what is the date
you have on the calendar

00:02:42.567 --> 00:02:43.734
for where you want to be
comfortable?

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:47.234
-It's March of 2079 is
when I'm going to

00:02:47.234 --> 00:02:48.901
start feeling comfortable, Seth.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:51.834
I think -- I'm very -- I don't
know if everybody else is,

00:02:51.834 --> 00:02:53.734
I'm very forgiving with myself
as a host.

00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:56.367
I'm like, "It's an evolution.
I messed up the teleprompter.

00:02:56.367 --> 00:02:57.501
I got the facts --"

00:02:57.501 --> 00:02:58.868
No, I don't -- We try not to get
the facts wrong.

00:02:58.868 --> 00:03:02.667
-Yeah. "I'm making it up!"
-"Just go with it!"

00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:06.300
You know, I think stepping into
the 9:00 p.m. slot on MSNBC,

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.234
previously occupied
Monday through Friday

00:03:08.234 --> 00:03:10.701
by the great Rachel Maddow...
-Yep.

00:03:10.701 --> 00:03:14.601
-...is a huge honor, and no one
can replicate what she does.

00:03:14.601 --> 00:03:17.934
And I think we wanted to figure
out what the DNA of our show is

00:03:17.934 --> 00:03:19.467
and that just takes some time.

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:21.067
We've tried some stuff
that's worked,

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.434
we've tried some stuff
that hasn't.

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:26.000
And I think luckily our audience
seems to be with us.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.501
People are forgiving.
People are excited to see,

00:03:28.501 --> 00:03:30.601
you know, the plane being built
on the runway.

00:03:30.601 --> 00:03:34.567
And I'm just thrilled that I'm
still the air six months later.

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:36.467
-Well, I feel the same way.

00:03:36.467 --> 00:03:38.534
One of the things you've
addressed, which I want to ask

00:03:38.534 --> 00:03:40.801
you about, because obviously
the Republicans are coming off

00:03:40.801 --> 00:03:42.734
sort of underwhelming midterms.

00:03:42.734 --> 00:03:44.367
But it does seem like they're

00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:48.133
still sticking with
this culture war message.

00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:51.734
And one of the people you see
using that as a tool more often

00:03:51.734 --> 00:03:54.167
than others is Florida governor
Ron DeSantis.

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:55.701
You've been covering the way

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:58.567
he's been approaching politics
via education.

00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:01.067
Can you speak to some of
the steps he's taking

00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:03.534
and maybe why we should be
afraid of those?

00:04:03.534 --> 00:04:05.968
-I mean, you should be afraid of
anything that systematically

00:04:05.968 --> 00:04:08.934
tries to disenfranchise people
and whitewash history,

00:04:08.934 --> 00:04:10.868
which is exactly what's
happening down in Florida.

00:04:10.868 --> 00:04:12.901
We should care about it not just
because it's Florida but also

00:04:12.901 --> 00:04:15.234
because Ron DeSantis may end up
being the Republican nominee

00:04:15.234 --> 00:04:16.934
for president in 2024.

00:04:16.934 --> 00:04:20.434
But basically what he's done is
this kind of Orwellian plot

00:04:20.434 --> 00:04:22.868
to censor what is being taught
in the classrooms

00:04:22.868 --> 00:04:24.834
through the passage
of various legislation,

00:04:24.834 --> 00:04:26.901
book bans and book approvals.

00:04:26.901 --> 00:04:27.934
I mean, I was talking to
a teacher

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:29.634
in Manatee County this week.

00:04:29.634 --> 00:04:32.734
They've literally had to paper
over their school libraries

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:35.868
and classrooms because the books
haven't all been approved

00:04:35.868 --> 00:04:37.667
to these state-set standards.

00:04:37.667 --> 00:04:40.934
Kids can't read books in
classrooms because teachers are

00:04:40.934 --> 00:04:44.467
so scared they could get slapped
with a felony for running afoul

00:04:44.467 --> 00:04:46.200
of these new standards.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.534
Ron DeSantis has said, "I'm not
going to approve an AP course

00:04:49.534 --> 00:04:51.634
on African-American studies
in the state of Florida

00:04:51.634 --> 00:04:53.968
because so far I've seen
no evidence

00:04:53.968 --> 00:04:56.200
that it has
any educational value."

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:00.667
I mean, this is the way we
reindoctrinate people to sort of

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:03.601
a white Christian nationalist
agenda, which is very much

00:05:03.601 --> 00:05:06.634
at the heart of the Republican
sort of platform,

00:05:06.634 --> 00:05:08.934
if you will, right now,
and Ron DeSantis has been

00:05:08.934 --> 00:05:13.000
insanely effective in passing
this legislation,

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.701
creating a climate of fear,
and ultimately undermining a lot

00:05:16.701 --> 00:05:18.634
of the social and cultural
progress that this country

00:05:18.634 --> 00:05:20.567
has seen in
the last 15 and 20 years.

00:05:20.567 --> 00:05:22.868
So I think we should all be
paying attention to it.

00:05:22.868 --> 00:05:25.934
I've gone down to Florida. I've
talked to teachers and students.

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:27.400
It's part of the field work that

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.367
I think is essential
to being a journalist.

00:05:29.367 --> 00:05:30.734
I'm surprised I'm still allowed

00:05:30.734 --> 00:05:32.501
to enter the state of Florida,
at this point.

00:05:32.501 --> 00:05:34.067
I hope you're not watching,
Governor DeSantis.

00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:35.467
-I don't think he is.

00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:37.200
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.400
-Probably not.
-Probably not, yeah.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.200
-Probably not.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.267
-"I like the monologue,
but that's it!"

00:05:42.267 --> 00:05:44.601
-"Then I turn it off!"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:47.501
You know, he's trying to take
the one -- There's one school,

00:05:47.501 --> 00:05:49.501
New College, down in Florida
that's the bastion

00:05:49.501 --> 00:05:50.968
of sort of progressive thought.

00:05:50.968 --> 00:05:53.734
He's trying to stack the board
of trustees at that college

00:05:53.734 --> 00:05:57.000
to basically reverse the
curriculum that they have there.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:01.501
I mean, everything he can do to
censor, marginalize,

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:05.667
whitewash, and otherwise create
a culture of

00:06:05.667 --> 00:06:09.133
intimidation around those who
want to tell the truth about

00:06:09.133 --> 00:06:11.200
this country and our systemic
injustices,

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.701
he's doing it, and he's doing it
with ruthless efficacy.

00:06:14.701 --> 00:06:17.167
And now you have other states
that are looking at Ron DeSantis

00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:19.133
and saying, "Maybe we should do
that too."

00:06:19.133 --> 00:06:21.267
South Carolina is looking at
some of the same legislation

00:06:21.267 --> 00:06:22.667
that they've passed in Florida.

00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:24.601
This is the new agenda
of the right.

00:06:24.601 --> 00:06:26.000
If you can't win fair,

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.133
win using any anti-democratic
means necessary.

00:06:29.133 --> 00:06:31.634
-This is -- I think it's --
This is less important,

00:06:31.634 --> 00:06:33.334
what I'm going to ask about
because obviously the outcome is

00:06:33.334 --> 00:06:35.601
the most important and you do
have teachers who are scared

00:06:35.601 --> 00:06:37.267
and you do have books
that are banned.

00:06:37.267 --> 00:06:40.033
Do you think -- I know it's kind
of impossible to know that

00:06:40.033 --> 00:06:42.868
Ron DeSantis came at this
because he was afraid of kids

00:06:42.868 --> 00:06:45.334
reading books, or he came at
this because he wanted to be

00:06:45.334 --> 00:06:47.167
the guy who could stand up at
a debate and say,

00:06:47.167 --> 00:06:48.567
"I'm the guy who banned books"?

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:51.934
-He wants to -- This is all
political calculus.

00:06:51.934 --> 00:06:55.200
This is -- I mean, Ron DeSantis,
if he could, the license plate

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.968
for Florida would say, "this is
where woke goes to die."

00:06:57.968 --> 00:06:59.634
That's all he says
about his state.

00:06:59.634 --> 00:07:01.367
That's like the selling --

00:07:01.367 --> 00:07:03.234
That's the motto for the state
of Florida.

00:07:03.234 --> 00:07:04.767
He knows it plays well

00:07:04.767 --> 00:07:06.767
with people on the right
who want a culture war.

00:07:06.767 --> 00:07:09.367
And so far, I mean,
he won handily

00:07:09.367 --> 00:07:12.000
in the last gubernatorial race.
It's worked for him.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.033
The question is whether
the extreme policies that

00:07:15.033 --> 00:07:16.901
he's pursued in the state of
Florida work well

00:07:16.901 --> 00:07:19.601
on the national stage if, in
fact, he does run for president

00:07:19.601 --> 00:07:22.868
against Donald Trump, which is a
cage match I don't know if I'm,

00:07:22.868 --> 00:07:25.100
like, physically, mentally
prepared for.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.334
-I don't think Ron DeSantis is
either.

00:07:27.334 --> 00:07:28.968
-That may be true, Seth.

00:07:28.968 --> 00:07:30.400
-I think from a distance,
you're like,

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:32.634
"I can take out Donald Trump,"
and then you get on a stage,

00:07:32.634 --> 00:07:33.734
and you're like "Oh, man, I
shouldn't have done this."

00:07:33.734 --> 00:07:35.701
-"Maybe not, yeah."

00:07:35.701 --> 00:07:38.534
-You have a big fan,
actually backstage.

00:07:38.534 --> 00:07:41.734
Our next guest, Bowen Yang,
sings your praises.

00:07:41.734 --> 00:07:43.467
Independent of you
being on tonight,

00:07:43.467 --> 00:07:44.501
he is a huge fan of yours.

00:07:44.501 --> 00:07:46.234
-It's a mutual admiration
society.

00:07:46.234 --> 00:07:49.267
-Yeah. Because you hosted the
reboot of the show "The Mole."

00:07:49.267 --> 00:07:51.901
-Yes, as one does.
-Yeah.

00:07:51.901 --> 00:07:53.334
Well, you know, this was
the show that's always been

00:07:53.334 --> 00:07:54.467
in the hands of journalists.

00:07:54.467 --> 00:07:56.234
Anderson Cooper did it back
in the day.

00:07:56.234 --> 00:07:58.234
And now you're doing it.
And was that a fun show to do?

00:07:58.234 --> 00:08:00.901
-It was not like covering the
insurrection at the Capitol.

00:08:00.901 --> 00:08:02.434
[ Laughter ]

00:08:02.434 --> 00:08:06.267
You know, it dawned on me that
some people in TV have fun.

00:08:06.267 --> 00:08:07.934
It's not that my job isn't fun,

00:08:07.934 --> 00:08:10.133
but, like, it's incredibly
serious.

00:08:10.133 --> 00:08:12.934
And it's a lot of traumatic
material to absorb every day.

00:08:12.934 --> 00:08:16.501
And I thought, "Oh, this could
be really a fun experience,"

00:08:16.501 --> 00:08:21.067
doing this crazy reboot that at
its core is a question of trust,

00:08:21.067 --> 00:08:23.434
which, you know, I will say
there's a through line between

00:08:23.434 --> 00:08:26.434
that and what we do in terms of
political coverage.

00:08:26.434 --> 00:08:29.400
The central conceit in American
life today is, who do you trust,

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:32.634
who can you trust, who's really
being honest here?

00:08:32.634 --> 00:08:34.567
And that's the game
in "The Mole."

00:08:34.567 --> 00:08:37.467
So I thought, "Okay, I can
create a reason why it makes

00:08:37.467 --> 00:08:39.968
sense for me to do this and go
to Australia for 10 weeks."

00:08:39.968 --> 00:08:41.801
-Well, I'm very glad you got
that trip,

00:08:41.801 --> 00:08:44.100
no matter how you justified it
to yourself.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:47.000
Hey, thank you so much for being
here. I really appreciate it.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.868
-Thanks, Seth.
-Alex Wagner.

00:08:48.868 --> 00:08:51.634
"Alex Wagner Tonight" airs
Tuesdays through Fridays

00:08:51.634 --> 00:08:55.133
at 9:00 p.m. on MSNBC. We'll be
right back with Bowen Yang!

